from reviewlib import review as R,reuse,save,reviews
R('b3-q1',None,'bundles,bundle-commands','Set a variable value; bundle deploy — target',
 'A bundle target is selected with --target, not --profile. However, the printed prod configuration spells the override datasetcatalog rather than dataset_catalog, so target selection alone cannot establish the requested variable override without repairing the source configuration.',
 'Profiles choose authentication; targets choose bundle environment settings; variable names must match exactly.',
 'An authentication profile named prod does not itself select targets.prod. The option incorrectly conflates credentials with the deployment target.',
 'B names the correct target-selection flag, but the printed variable-key mismatch remains. If dataset_catalog was intended in prod, B would be the conceptual answer after that source correction.',
 'bundle execute is not the documented command, and --profile still does not choose the bundle target. Both parts fail to implement the requested deployment.',
 '--var can override dataset_catalog’s value but does not automatically select targets.prod. It cannot establish the claimed target choice by itself.',status='inconsistent-variable-key')
R('b3-q2','a','job-history,spark-ui','Run duration; Run/task details',
 'Compare run durations over time and drill into the affected run/task compute metrics to distinguish consistent regression from intermittent contention. Historical data is essential to that comparison, not something to discard.',
 'Measure trend first, then correlate the slow run with task and compute evidence.',
 'A uses recorded run and resource evidence to investigate the regression. Pair the run-duration trend with detailed task/compute inspection rather than assume every delay is caused by a resource spike.',
 'Archiving old runs removes the evidence needed to compare against the baseline. Resetting a counter does not diagnose the underlying slowdown.',
 'There is no universal rule making seven-day-old run information irrelevant. Rerunning alone cannot determine whether the prior week’s slowdown was consistent.',
 'Automatically increasing cluster size before diagnosis can add cost without fixing skew, I/O, or code regressions. A slower run is evidence to investigate, not proof of undersized hardware.')
R('b3-q6','c','bundle-commands,bundles-overview','bundle generate; bundle deployment bind',
 'bundle generate exports supported existing resources into configuration and --bind associates that configuration with existing workspace objects. Without binding, deploying a generated resource can create a new object instead of updating the original.',
 'Generation writes configuration; binding establishes which existing resource it manages.',
 'init creates a project and deploy does not automatically discover every existing workspace resource. The proposed implicit import is not the documented workflow.',
 'REST-exported JSON can be versioned and replayed but does not itself create the requested bundle project. It uses a different lower-level deployment scheme.',
 'Generate the existing job/dashboard resource configuration and bind it to the original IDs. Committing that source/configuration gives the team a reproducible bundle-managed project.',
 'Manually recreating YAML can define new resources, but without explicit binding it may deploy duplicates. The option does not establish management of the original resource identities.')
R('b3-q7','b','pipeline-events,expectations','Query data quality or expectations metrics',
 'The pipeline event log contains structured quality metrics keyed by update, dataset, and expectation. Filter the relevant update and aggregate failed-record counts to locate the most frequently violated rule and affected table.',
 'Failed expectation records under DROP ROW are quality metrics, not necessarily failed Spark tasks.',
 'Notebook HTML output is not the authoritative structured pipeline expectation dataset. Exporting rendered output adds manual parsing instead of using the built-in event log.',
 'B queries the pipeline event log for the latest update’s expectation metrics. The documented flow_progress quality fields include dataset/name and passed/failed counts.',
 'Physical query plans do not list each rejected business-rule row and expectation metric as the requested report. Spark UI stages are a different diagnostic layer.',
 'Increasing driver log verbosity is unnecessary for already captured structured quality events. Log4j text parsing is less direct than the event-log metrics.')
R('b3-q8','d','spark-ui,memory,job-history','Spark UI guide; Stage/task metrics',
 'Spark UI is the detailed execution view for jobs, stages, tasks, shuffle, spills, and executor information on supported classic compute. High-level run timelines and hardware charts complement rather than replace that stage/task analysis.',
 'Use the tool at the granularity of the question: task metrics require Spark execution detail.',
 'Compute metrics show resource behavior but not the complete stage/task query-execution detail requested. Excluding Spark UI removes the direct diagnostic view.',
 'Lakeflow run timelines show task orchestration, not every Spark stage/task metric by themselves. The option’s without Spark UI limitation is too restrictive.',
 'Ganglia-style hardware monitoring is not the full Spark stage/task/shuffle analysis interface. Hardware trends alone do not identify the regressed stage logic.',
 'D opens the Spark UI for the run’s compute and inspects Jobs and Stages. This is the documented starting point for the requested detailed Spark execution diagnosis.')
reuse('b3-q9','b2-q34','cadb','d')
R('b3-q10','a','repair','Repair an unsuccessful job run',
 'Repair uses current job/task settings and records the repair as part of the run’s recovery history. It is appropriate for rerunning failed/skipped work after updating the task path and cluster configuration.',
 'A new Run now execution does not replace the prior run or preserve its same repair lineage.',
 'A uses the repair-run operation after changing the job configuration. The reference explains that repairs use current job/task settings and appear in run history.',
 'Retries are failure-policy attempts and are not the documented explicit repair workflow for revised configuration. Increasing retry count alone does not establish the intended recovery selection/history.',
 'Redeploying a bundle changes job resources but does not automatically repair the recorded failed execution. An explicit repair operation is still needed.',
 'Run now starts a distinct new run and does not replace the original history. It also does not automatically preserve successful task results from the failed run.')
R('b3-q11','b','bundle-settings','Bundle configuration file',
 'The bundle’s root configuration is databricks.yml and can define name, targets, variables, resources, and includes. Source files may be split into included configuration, but the root file remains the entry point.',
 'A declarative bundle configuration is different from a runtime script that creates resources imperatively.',
 'A Python script can automate API calls but is not the required root bundle configuration file. It does not substitute for databricks.yml.',
 'B identifies databricks.yml with the supported bundle metadata and resource/target settings. This is the documented main configuration entry point.',
 'Text descriptions are not parsed as resource definitions by the bundle CLI. A directory of prose files does not supply the required schema.',
 'bundle_config.json is not the documented root filename. A JSON-shaped resource description does not change the required bundle entry point.')
R('b3-q12','b','bundle-settings','Bundle configuration; include',
 'A bundle has one root databricks.yml entry point and can include other configuration files. Included files provide modularity without making multiple competing root configurations.',
 'The include mechanism extends one root project; it is not arbitrary automatic merging of independent bundle roots.',
 'Multiple independent databricks.yml roots in subfolders are not the required authoritative root. Additional resource files can be included without making each one a separate root bundle.',
 'B matches the documented root entry point plus include mechanism. It supports a standardized project while permitting modular resource definitions.',
 'A hidden databricks directory is not the only valid or required root location. The CLI expects the project root configuration.',
 'The claimed .yaml-over-.yml priority and competing subfolder roots are not the documented project convention. Use the required databricks.yml entry point.')
R('b3-q13','a','serverless-jobs,photon','Requirements; Serverless compute',
 'Serverless jobs compute manages instance provisioning, scaling, runtime lifecycle, and supported acceleration for eligible workloads. Unity Catalog and regional availability are documented prerequisites.',
 'A SQL warehouse is not a general Python notebook job-compute product.',
 'A selects the compute product designed to abstract those cluster-management decisions. Check supported task, library, networking, and language constraints before migrating.',
 'A Pro SQL warehouse does not generally execute arbitrary Python notebook tasks as classic job compute. It is the wrong task/compute interface for this requirement.',
 'Manually choosing instance families and worker bounds retains the configuration responsibility the team wants to remove. A single-task cluster does not automatically become serverless.',
 'Policies standardize classic compute but still leave a managed cluster model with sizing/runtime choices. They do not provide the same serverless lifecycle abstraction.')
R('b3-q14',None,'repair','Single-task job recovery',
 'The official repair reference says single-task jobs must be triggered again with Run now; repair is supported only for two or more tasks. This version of the source question omits rerun/Run now from its options.',
 'Do not choose Repair run merely because a similar multi-task question uses it.',
 'Repair the task is not the supported single-task recovery action in the reference. A new job run is needed after the notebook fix.',
 'Repair run is specifically limited to jobs orchestrating at least two tasks. It is not valid for the single-task scenario stated here.',
 'Restarting compute does not resubmit the corrected job. It can add startup work without producing a new run.',
 'Swapping compute likewise does not execute the corrected notebook. No demonstrated compute defect requires that change.',status='missing-single-task-rerun-option')
R('b3-q15',None,'job-schedules,compute','Schedule jobs; Job compute lifecycle',
 'C describes a potential cost benefit of scheduled job-scoped compute, but none of the options configures or verifies the schedule itself. Ensuring scheduled triggers requires a schedule, time zone, run admission settings, and monitoring, not one of these descriptive assertions.',
 'A benefit of a schedule is not a method for validating that the schedule actually fired.',
 'Scheduled jobs need not keep an always-running cluster. Job-scoped compute can start for executions and terminate afterward.',
 'Processing data as it arrives describes an event-driven or continuous pattern, not the definition of a wall-clock schedule. A schedule runs at configured times.',
 'C is a reasonable benefit when using job-scoped compute, but it does not answer how to ensure scheduled triggers occur. The stem and choices ask different questions.',
 'Running continuously until stopped describes continuous execution. It is not required for a scheduled batch job.',status='stem-and-answer-type-mismatch')
R('b3-q16','a','pipeline-sql,ldp-python,medallion','SQL and Python pipeline development',
 'A pipeline may include SQL and Python source definitions while preserving medallion layers and streaming inputs. Existing code still needs supported dataset definitions and dependencies rather than a forced single-language rewrite.',
 'Source languages can be mixed across pipeline definitions; a single notebook cell still has one execution language.',
 'A correctly states that SQL and Python notebook/source definitions can coexist. It preserves the engineer’s and analyst’s language choices.',
 'There is no SQL-only restriction on the managed pipeline. Python dataset definitions are explicitly documented.',
 'Streaming input is supported by streaming tables and Structured Streaming. Replacing it with batch input is not required.',
 'There is no Python-only restriction either. SQL transformations can remain part of the pipeline dataflow.')
R('b3-q17','c','compute,single-node','Jobs compute; All-purpose compute',
 'A tested Python notebook scheduled in production fits job-scoped compute rather than an interactive all-purpose cluster or SQL-only warehouse. Runtime and Unity Catalog compatibility are separate necessary checks.',
 'Choose lifecycle by interactive versus automated work, then configure governance and resources.',
 'All-purpose compute is useful for interactive development but can carry idle cost between scheduled runs. It is not the most direct production job-scoped lifecycle offered.',
 'Unity Catalog enablement is a governance compatibility condition, not a sufficient compute lifecycle choice. It does not distinguish interactive from scheduled execution.',
 'Jobs compute is designed to execute automated tasks under a job lifecycle. It can provision for the run and terminate when the scheduled work completes.',
 'A serverless SQL warehouse is not a general execution environment for arbitrary Python notebook ingestion code. The job-compute product must match the task type.')
