R('b3-q35','d','streaming','processingTime: Time-based trigger intervals',
 'trigger(processingTime="5 seconds") requests fixed-interval micro-batch scheduling. A batch that takes longer than its interval does not create a guarantee of five-second end-to-end latency.',
 'The micro-batch interval keyword is processingTime; Once and Continuous are different modes.',
 'A omits the named trigger argument and is not the documented Python call form. A bare string does not select the requested mode.',
 'Continuous is not the fixed-interval micro-batch trigger requested and is not the supported Databricks mode in this reference. It cannot be substituted merely because it also accepts an interval-looking value.',
 'once expects a Boolean bounded-run flag, not a five-second interval. It does not schedule successive micro-batches every five seconds.',
 'D uses the supported processingTime keyword and duration string. It directly describes the requested micro-batch trigger interval.')
R('b3-q36','d','streaming','processingTime: Time-based trigger intervals',
 'A fixed micro-batch interval uses trigger(processingTime="5 seconds"). Default scheduling and bounded Once execution do not express that configured interval.',
 'Distinguish scheduling frequency from the duration a batch actually takes to finish.',
 'A does not use the required named trigger parameter. The reference shows processingTime for a fixed-interval micro-batch trigger.',
 'An unspecified trigger uses default micro-batch behavior rather than explicitly waiting on the requested five-second period. It does not encode the scenario’s interval.',
 'once is a Boolean single-batch/bounded mode, not a duration field. This value does not implement repeated five-second processing.',
 'D is the documented fixed-interval micro-batch syntax. If processing exceeds five seconds, the runtime cannot promise a five-second completion latency.',
 'Continuous is a different, unsupported mode in the cited Databricks trigger overview. It is not the requested micro-batch processingTime configuration.')
R('b3-q37',None,'null-safe,sql-null','Null-safe equality; Comparison operators',
 'The documented null-safe equality operator is the contiguous token <=>. The source prints <= > with internal whitespace; B, C, and D do not preserve the exact requested NULL/equality semantics, so A needs a syntax correction before being executable.',
 'Replacing NULL with a sentinel can create false matches with real sentinel-valued records.',
 'A intends the null-safe equality operator but prints it as two separated tokens. With the contiguous <=> spelling it would correctly match equal non-null values and two NULLs.',
 'Ordinary equality returns unknown when either operand is NULL. It does not make two NULL values match as the question requires.',
 'COALESCE with an empty-string sentinel also makes NULL equal to an actual empty string. That changes the required equality semantics for legitimate empty-string values.',
 'Inequality plus a left-side NULL test matches many unequal pairs and does not express symmetric null-safe equality. It is a different predicate.',status='split-null-safe-operator')
R('b3-q38','b','join','INNER JOIN; LEFT OUTER JOIN',
 'An inner join on customer_id retains orders whose customer key appears in the active-customer DataFrame. Validate uniqueness of the active-customer keys to avoid multiplying orders; a left-semi join would also be a useful unlisted alternative when only order columns are needed.',
 'Use a membership-preserving join/filter rather than retain unmatched orders.',
 'Starting from customers with a left join preserves every active customer, including those without orders. It does not directly return only the matching orders set.',
 'An orders-to-active-customers inner join on the customer key removes orders without an active customer match. This implements the stated membership filter among the choices.',
 'A cross join creates all row combinations and does not test customer membership. It can massively expand the dataset.',
 'A left join from orders retains orders with no active-customer match unless a further filter is added. No such filter is included in the printed operation.')
R('b3-q39','c','materialized,views','What are standalone materialized views?',
 'A materialized view physically stores a query result and can refresh it as source data changes or on a schedule. It suits expensive BI aggregates whose results should be precomputed rather than recomputed for each reader.',
 'Precomputed result storage distinguishes a materialized view from an ordinary view.',
 'A streaming table focuses on incremental source processing and is not automatically the best representation for arbitrary expensive BI aggregates. The requirement is precomputed serving results.',
 'External table describes storage ownership, not whether a complex query result is precomputed and refreshed. It does not directly satisfy the object behavior requested.',
 'C provides stored aggregate results with a managed refresh lifecycle. That reduces repeated dashboard query work for slowly changing source data.',
 'An ordinary view stores the query definition and computes it when queried. It does not itself provide the required persisted precomputation.')
R('b3-q40','b','skew','Skew — Compare Max and the 75th percentile',
 'The current Spark UI guide flags possible skew when maximum task duration exceeds the 75th percentile by at least 50%. Here 80 seconds is 60% above 50 seconds, so skew is the documented suspicion, not proof of a particular root cause.',
 'Use the documented comparison: Max versus the 75th percentile, not simply Max versus the median.',
 'Task durations alone do not identify garbage collection as the cause. Inspect GC metrics before making that specific diagnosis.',
 '80/50 = 1.6, which exceeds the guide’s 1.5-times screening threshold. The stage may have skew and merits partition/input-size investigation.',
 'The documented skew screening condition is met, so healthy without further investigation is not the supported interpretation. It is still a diagnostic indication rather than certainty.',
 'Under-provisioning is a capacity diagnosis that requires additional resource and task evidence. The duration tail alone does not establish it.')
R('b3-q41','d','connect,memory','Local materialization of Spark results; Memory troubleshooting',
 'toPandas collects distributed results into driver-side/local memory in the execution context. A wide 50-million-row result can exceed driver capacity even if executors successfully processed all partitions.',
 'More distributed worker memory does not make an all-rows collection fit on one driver.',
 'The storage/execution fraction is not the direct memory budget for holding the collected pandas result. Tweaking it is not the central driver collection issue.',
 'Executor memory is relevant to distributed tasks, but the reported failure is on the driver during collection. Increasing workers does not enlarge the driver heap.',
 'Shuffle partition count affects distributed task sizing, not the final amount collected onto one driver. It cannot make an unbounded full result small enough by itself.',
 'D identifies the driver memory boundary for the collected result. Prefer avoiding or reducing the collection before simply allocating enough memory for every row.')
R('b3-q42','d','join-hints,shuffle','BROADCAST hint; Broadcast join with AQE',
 'Broadcasting a suitably small lookup lets each executor join its large-table partitions against a local copy rather than shuffle the large side for a sort-merge join. Confirm actual byte size and join support; row count alone is not the broadcast memory limit.',
 'Broadcast the small dimension, not the billion-row fact dataset.',
 'Caching can help repeated reuse but does not itself change the join strategy or eliminate the large shuffle. It is not the direct optimization requested.',
 'Persisting has the same limitation: it stores reusable input but does not force a broadcast strategy. The shuffle can remain.',
 'Broadcasting billions of events can overwhelm executor/driver memory and network. It chooses the wrong side of the highly asymmetric join.',
 'Broadcast the small country lookup when its size is safely within the supported budget. This directly targets avoiding a costly shuffle of the large events side.')
R('b3-q43','c','join-hints,shuffle','Broadcast planning; AQE broadcast threshold',
 'spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold controls the static optimizer’s automatic broadcast eligibility by estimated size. Databricks AQE has a separate adaptive threshold; neither setting should be raised without checking actual lookup size and executor memory.',
 'Static broadcast planning and adaptive broadcast conversion have different configuration names.',
 'Shuffle partitions controls partitioning for shuffle work. It is not the size threshold deciding automatic static broadcast eligibility.',
 'Executor memory sets a resource budget but does not by itself select the optimizer’s join broadcast threshold. It remains a safety constraint when choosing one.',
 'C is the static Spark SQL automatic broadcast threshold. The separate Databricks adaptive threshold applies to runtime AQE decisions.',
 'Default parallelism is not the automatic small-table broadcast size limit. Adjusting it does not directly select the desired join strategy.')
R('b3-q44','b','join','LEFT ANTI JOIN; Projection',
 'A left anti join returns only left-side customer rows with no matching order key, and includes no order columns. It directly expresses non-membership without requiring a later projection cleanup.',
 'SEMI means left rows with a match; ANTI means left rows without one.',
 'An inner join only contains matched customers, so filtering it for a missing order cannot recover customers with no orders. It starts from the wrong matched population.',
 'A left anti join from customers to orders returns precisely customers absent from the order keys. Its output contains only customer columns.',
 'A left join starting from orders preserves orders, not customers with no orders. It cannot discover a customer absent from that left input.',
 'A full outer join with a null filter would still include order-related columns unless explicitly projected away and can have null-key subtleties. The printed expression does not exactly meet the customer-only output requirement.')
R('b3-q45',None,'sql-null,dataframes','IS NULL semantics; Filter rows in a DataFrame',
 'The needed row rule is that both email and phone are non-null; dropna with an appropriate subset is the intended direct API. A correctly spelled filter with both predicates can also express the rule in one DataFrame operation, while the source’s D method spelling is malformed, making the single-call distinction and code quality unclear.',
 'A row with either missing value must be removed; dropping only all-null rows is a different rule.',
 'A is the intended subset-based null removal and normally drops rows with any null in those two fields. The retained official pages establish the predicate semantics but not a complete standalone dropna API reference.',
 'B chains two where operations, contrary to the explicit single method-call constraint, and its printed quotes are inconsistent. It does not meet the requested form.',
 'how=all removes rows only when every relevant value is null and omits the requested subset. A row missing only email or phone could remain.',
 'A correctly spelled filter with both isNotNull predicates is logically valid as one DataFrame filtering operation. The printed isNotNul11 typo and ambiguity over whether expression-method calls count prevent using it as a clean exclusive distractor.',status='api-reference-and-single-call-ambiguity')
R('b3-q46','a','dedup','Deduplicate specific columns',
 'dropDuplicates with the customer_id/email subset compares the business keys while retaining one input row’s remaining columns. It does not guarantee that the retained timestamp is the latest; use an explicit ordering/window rule if that is required.',
 'Key-based row deduplication differs from aggregating or projecting away non-key data.',
 'A passes the two business keys as the deduplication subset. Differences in ingestion_timestamp no longer make otherwise duplicate customer records distinct.',
 'B creates an aggregated key/timestamp result and does not generally retain an original full record’s other attributes. It is appropriate when a latest-timestamp aggregate is the desired output, which is not asked for.',
 'C returns only the two projected key columns. It removes duplicates but changes the record schema rather than retaining one representative input record.',
 'Full-row distinct compares ingestion_timestamp too, so rows differing in that field remain. It does not use the specified business-key equivalence.')
R('b3-q47',None,'jdbc,lineage,create-table','JDBC reads; saveAsTable; Lineage limitations',
 'Writing a Spark JDBC DataFrame with saveAsTable creates a catalog-addressable table, but full external-source lineage is not guaranteed for every JDBC read by that write call. The requirement for full lineage needs the documented external lineage support or explicit external metadata capture for the actual source.',
 'Registering a target table and capturing its entire external provenance are separate claims.',
 'Staging through REST and a second load is not direct catalog landing and does not automatically capture source lineage. It adds another processing boundary.',
 'B is the intended direct JDBC-to-catalog write and supports immediate SQL access after commit. It does not universally establish the claimed full legacy-database lineage solely from saveAsTable.',
 'Collecting through pandas can lose distributed scalability and writing an external path does not register a Unity Catalog table by itself. The requested named-table landing is not established.',
 'ODBC pooling and /dbfs file output do not define the governed catalog table or its complete lineage. They are connection/storage mechanics rather than the required governance guarantee.',status='full-external-lineage-not-established')
R('b3-q48','a','materialized,medallion','Materialized views; Power analytics with the gold layer',
 'A materialized view stores business-ready aggregate results and refreshes them under managed pipeline semantics. This serves consistent, fast BI results rather than asking every report to rebuild raw joins.',
 'Gold is a serving model; raw event ingestion alone is not a business-ready aggregate.',
 'A precomputes the Silver-derived business measures and exposes a reusable result for dashboards. Keep any notebook logic compatible with the supported materialized-view/pipeline definition interface.',
 'Streaming raw events directly to BI skips the stated validated business-ready layer. A streaming table alone does not define the required aggregate metrics.',
 'A view over raw Bronze with each BI client defining its own logic risks inconsistent definitions and repeated work. It does not meet the precomputed shared-serving goal.',
 'A folder of CSV files makes readers parse and model data themselves and lacks the requested governed reusable query object. It is not the intended Gold serving design.')
R('b3-q49','a','complex-data','Dot notation for accessing nested data; Select all nested fields',
 'Selecting explicit dotted leaf paths flattens the nested fields into result columns while preserving event_id and event_ts. A star expands only one struct depth and therefore leaves a deeper location struct intact.',
 'One-level device.* expansion is not recursive flattening.',
 'A explicitly selects event identifiers plus device.id, device.model, and the two location leaf fields. The result contains scalar leaf columns rather than the nested location struct.',
 'B adds a root location column whose value is still the location struct. It does not fully flatten that second nested level and also retains existing columns unless projected.',
 'C expands the immediate device fields, including location as a struct. The official complex-type reference explicitly says star expansion unpacks only the specified depth.')
R('b3-q50','b','memory,skew,connect','Driver collection; Spill and executor memory',
 'collect materializes the full result on the driver, while a wide shuffle can exhaust executor task memory. Treat the two memory boundaries separately and avoid unnecessary full-data collection before relying on larger allocations.',
 'Driver memory and per-executor/task memory are not interchangeable.',
 'Storage-optimized versus compute-optimized labels do not specifically address the reported driver collection and executor heap boundaries. Resource type should follow the measured memory demand.',
 'B correctly targets driver capacity for collection and partition sizing or executor memory for shuffle pressure. Reducing the collected data is usually safer than attempting to collect an entire 100-GB result.',
 'More executors do not increase the memory of the driver collecting all results. More driver memory likewise does not fix an executor’s oversized shuffle partition.',
 'AQE can change distributed join/partition strategies but does not automatically allocate unlimited driver or executor heap. It cannot make an unbounded collect safe by itself.')
R('b3-q51',None,'from-json,get-json,medallion','from_json; get_json_object; Silver data modeling',
 'from_json can persist a typed struct once in Silver; get_json_object with correct paths and casts can also persist flattened fields once. The question does not require a struct versus flat columns or a specific age type, so C and D can both meet its literal downstream-query goal when materialized.',
 'Distinguish parsing at Silver write time from repeatedly parsing the original JSON string at query time.',
 'A uses field strings without the documented JSONPath root syntax. It also returns extracted values as strings rather than declaring a typed structured representation.',
 'B uses struct-style field access on a STRING column. JSON text must first be parsed or extracted; dot notation alone does not turn text into a struct.',
 'C parses the JSON string into a declared struct and can store it for nested-field queries without repeated text parsing. Its age is declared STRING, so an integer-age requirement would need an additional schema/cast choice.',
 'D extracts supported $. paths and casts age to INT, yielding flat columns that can be stored in Silver. Once materialized, those columns also avoid downstream JSON parsing, so the supplied D key is not uniquely required by this stem.',status='multiple-valid-materialized-json-shapes')
R('b3-q52','c','dedup','Deduplicate specific columns',
 'Specify user_id and event_type as the dropDuplicates subset to keep one representative input row per business-key pair. Without a deterministic ordering rule, the chosen event_timestamp is not guaranteed to be the newest.',
 'The comparison key can differ from the complete output-row schema.',
 'A returns grouped counts rather than one retained original record. It changes the output schema and purpose from deduplication to aggregation.',
 'B performs full-row distinct before projecting keys, so rows differing only by timestamp can remain duplicated after projection. The projection itself does not add another distinct step.',
 'C compares the specified business keys and retains one row for each pair. Other differing fields do not prevent those rows from being considered duplicates.',
 'D drops timestamp from the output and then compares every remaining field. It changes the schema and does not generally express the requested two-key equivalence.')
R('b3-q53','a','group-by,sum-function','Grouped aggregation; Multiple aggregate expressions',
 'Group by customer_id once and supply sum, average, and maximum expressions together to agg. The expression functions must be imported from the appropriate Spark SQL functions module in executable code.',
 'Multiple measures belong in one grouped aggregation call, not a sequence that repeatedly aggregates away earlier output.',
 'A groups by customer and supplies all three aggregate expressions together. It produces one row per customer with the required measures.',
 'GroupedData does not use select as the aggregation operation in this form. The named aggregation API is agg with supported expressions.',
 'C attempts to pass groupBy as an argument to transactions.agg rather than creating the grouping from the DataFrame. It does not define the intended grouped result.',
 'Chaining sum, avg, and max as shown is malformed and does not retain a common original grouped input for all measures. Use one agg call containing all three expressions.')
R('b3-q54','c,e','window,group-by,join','Window functions; Grouping and joins',
 'Window functions compute rolling/ranking metrics over partitioned ordered frames, while grouped totals joined to detail/group results can produce percent-of-total measures. A seven-day time window must use appropriate time-based bounds, not assume seven rows means seven days.',
 'Ranking and rolling windows differ from the numerator/denominator aggregation used for share-of-total calculations.',
 'RDD aggregation is not inherently faster than optimized DataFrame expressions. Dropping to manual RDD logic is not the recommended default for these supported analytical operations.',
 'Manual mapPartitions logic adds state, ordering, and boundary work that supported window functions already handle. It is not needed to express the rolling/ranking metrics.',
 'Window functions directly express rolling frames and ranks/top-N conditions. Choose a frame consistent with calendar-time rather than blindly using a fixed row count.',
 'Collecting the full DataFrame onto the driver loses distributed scalability and can exhaust driver memory. It is not a safe generic performance technique.',
 'groupBy can compute category/customer numerators and an overall denominator, and a suitable join or window can combine them. This is an appropriate distributed way to calculate percent-of-total metrics.')
